{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017oc8jvWC7XsqhEHvdKHv7D","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# From Requirements to Tables\n     2→\n     3→Use this reference when writing tests from natural-language requirements, vague\n     4→feature descriptions, or when it is not clear how to decompose the behaviour\n     5→into tables.\n     6→\n     7→**Your deliverable is a Java test class with `@TableTest` methods.**\n     8→Do not output markdown tables. Do not stop for user feedback between steps.\n     9→Work through every step below, then write the Java class directly as your\n    10→final output.\n    11→\n    12→Each step below is an analysis step that shapes the table structure. As you\n    13→work through them, you are building up a mental model of the columns, rows,\n    14→and concerns — but you do not output anything until step 9, where you write\n    15→the complete Java `@TableTest` class.\n    16→\n    17→---\n    18→\n    19→## 1. Name Each Concern\n    20→\n    21→Start by identifying the distinct behaviours in the requirement. Name each one\n    22→as a verb phrase:\n    23→\n    24→- \"Calculate weekly pay\"\n    25→- \"Determine discount tier\"\n    26→- \"Check traveller eligibility\"\n    27→- \"Validate coupon\"\n    28→\n    29→**If you cannot name a behaviour without using \"and\", it is two concerns** —\n    30→split them. Each concern becomes a candidate for its own `@TableTest` method.\n    31→\n    32→Signs that concerns are mixed:\n    33→- Some rows need columns that other rows leave blank throughout\n    34→- Scenario names require qualifiers like \"...for eligibility\" vs \"...for pricing\"\n    35→- The table has two groups of output columns that never both apply in the same row\n    36→\n    37→**Signs of a missing concern:**\n    38→- An input to one rule is itself derived from raw data. The recognisable\n    39→  shape: a rule takes a value that is computed via a time window, rolling\n    40→  count, aggregation, or filter over raw records (e.g. \"average response\n    41→  time over the last 7 days\" feeds into an SLA compliance check). The\n    42→  derivation has its own edge cases — what falls inside vs outside the\n    43→  window, how the boundary instant is handled, what records are included —\n    44→  and these need boundary testing in a separate table. The rule table then\n    45→  takes the derived value as a direct input column, not the raw data.\n    46→\n    47→  Two tables, not one: *Concern 1 — derive the value* (which raw records\n    48→  count, window boundaries). *Concern 2 — apply the rule* (given the\n    49→  derived value, what is the outcome?).\n    50→\n    51→---\n    52→\n    53→## 2. Find the First Example\n    54→\n    55→For each concern, start with the simplest, most obvious case:\n    56→\n    57→- What does a typical successful case look like?\n    58→- What are the concrete values — not abstractions, but real numbers, names, dates?\n    59→\n    60→Write this as the first data row. Column naming can be rough at this stage.\n    61→\n    62→---\n    63→\n    64→## 3. Probe for Variations\n    65→\n    66→Work through these systematically:\n    67→\n    68→**Different outcomes** — what makes the decision go the other way?\n    69→- \"What makes the answer change from yes to no?\"\n    70→- \"What other rules apply?\"\n    71→\n    72→**Boundary conditions** — where exactly do rules trigger?\n    73→- \"At what exact value does this rule kick in?\"\n    74→- Include rows at the threshold, just above, and just below\n    75→- When a boundary is ambiguous (inclusive vs exclusive?), add rows on both sides\n    76→  AND mark the expected output as an open question — the rows should exist even\n    77→  if the outcome is uncertain\n    78→\n    79→**Special cases** — situations that may surprise:\n    80→- \"Is there a case that surprises new team members?\"\n    81→- \"What is a common misunderstanding about this behaviour?\"\n    82→\n    83→**Absent inputs** — what happens when information is not provided?\n    84→- \"What if this field is empty or not given?\"\n    85→- \"Is there a sensible default, or does absence cause rejection?\"\n    86→\n    87→---\n    88→\n    89→## 4. Probe for Irrelevant Inputs\n    90→\n    91→When one input clearly does not affect the outcome, use a value set to express\n    92→\"regardless of\":\n    93→\n    94→```\n    95→Scenario                        | Customer Age | Car Category       | Eligible?\n    96→Underage regardless of category | 17           | {Economy, Premium} | no\n    97→```\n    98→\n    99→This is more precise than a blank (which means absent) and more concise than\n   100→separate rows for each category.\n   101→\n   102→Check every input dimension mentioned in the requirements. If the requirement\n   103→says \"regardless of X\", X must appear as a column with a value set — omitting\n   104→it silently hides the assertion that X is irrelevant.\n   105→\n   106→**Common mistake — omitting the irrelevant column entirely.** If a requirement\n   107→says \"regardless of membership level\", the table must include a Membership\n   108→Level column with a value set like `{Gold, Silver, Bronze}`. Without it, a\n   109→reader cannot tell whether the irrelevance was tested or simply overlooked.\n   110→The column makes the \"regardless of\" assertion visible and verifiable.\n   111→\n   112→**Value sets for tier grouping:** When multiple input values produce the same\n   113→output (a tier), group them into a value set rather than enumerating each as\n   114→a separate row:\n   115→\n   116→```\n   117→Scenario   | Credit Hours         | Standing?\n   118→Freshman   | {0, 10, 20, 29}     | Freshman\n   119→Sophomore  | {30, 45, 59}        | Sophomore\n   120→Junior     | {60, 75, 89}        | Junior\n   121→Senior     | {90, 100, 120}      | Senior\n   122→```\n   123→\n   124→This makes the tier structure a first-class concept — each row IS a tier. The\n   125→alternative — separate rows for boundaries (29 → Freshman, 30 → Sophomore) —\n   126→tests boundary detection but obscures which values belong to the same tier. Use\n   127→value sets for tier membership; add boundary rows (29 vs 30) only as additional\n   128→rows alongside the tier rows, not instead of them.\n   129→\n   130→**Value sets for identical entity types:** When two categories follow\n   131→identical rules (e.g. `{Manager, Director}` both have the same expense\n   132→approval limit), express them as a value set in one row rather than separate\n   133→rows. This is more concise AND more expressive — it asserts the rules are\n   134→identical, whereas separate rows merely happen to show the same output.\n   135→\n   136→---\n   137→\n   138→## 5. Separate Rules from Arithmetic\n   139→\n   140→Tables should specify the interesting decisions — classifications, eligibility\n   141→rules, tier lookups, state transitions — not test that multiplication works.\n   142→\n   143→**Signs you are testing arithmetic, not rules:**\n   144→- Every row has the same decision outcome but different numbers\n   145→- The table is testing that `a * b = c` for various values of a and b\n   146→- Removing rows would not lose any rule coverage\n   147→\n   148→When arithmetic is the only thing left to test, a small table (2-3 rows)\n   149→verifying the formula is sufficient. The bulk of the table rows should cover the\n   150→domain rules.\n   151→\n   152→**Good decomposition** — separate the rule from the calculation:\n   153→\n   154→Table 1 — the rule (which tier?):\n   155→```\n   156→Scenario     | Weight | Zone          | Category?  | Surcharge?\n   157→Light domestic | 0.5  | Domestic      | Standard   | none\n   158→Heavy domestic | 12   | Domestic      | Oversize   | 5.00\n   159→```\n   160→\n   161→Table 2 — the arithmetic (what does it cost?):\n   162→```\n   163→Scenario    | Base rate | Surcharge | Total?\n   164→No surcharge | 10.00    |           | 10.00\n   165→With surcharge | 10.00  | 5.00      | 15.00\n   166→```\n   167→\n   168→---\n   169→\n   170→## 6. Frame Stateful Features as Rules\n   171→\n   172→When a feature involves state (carts, orders, workflows), frame each row as a\n   173→state transition rule — not a step in a sequential path:\n   174→\n   175→```\n   176→Scenario              | Cart before    | Action         | Cart after?    | Message?\n   177→Add to empty cart     | []             | add Widget     | [Widget x1]    | Added\n   178→Remove last item      | [Widget x1]    | remove Widget  | []             | Removed\n   179→Remove unknown item   | [Widget x1]    | remove Gadget  | [Widget x1]    | Not found\n   180→```\n   181→\n   182→Each row is independent: given this state, when this action happens, expect this\n   183→result. No row depends on a previous row's outcome.\n   184→\n   185→**Include before and after columns** — even when the prompt describes the\n   186→operation procedurally (\"customers can enter a coupon code\") rather than as a\n   187→state transition. Whenever an operation changes system state, the reader needs to\n   188→see what changed.\n   189→\n   190→---\n   191→\n   192→## 7. Make Thresholds Visible\n   193→\n   194→When a rule depends on a threshold or limit, include it as a column — even when\n   195→the value is constant across every row:\n   196→\n   197→```\n   198→Scenario            | Customer Age | Max Age (Policy) | Eligible?\n   199→Standard customer   | 30           | 75               | yes\n   200→At the limit        | 75           | 75               | yes\n   201→Just over the limit | 76           | 75               | no\n   202→```\n   203→\n   204→A constant column often signals configuration. Ask: \"Under what circumstances\n   205→would this value differ?\" The answer may reveal a second axis (e.g., max age\n   206→varies by car category) that belongs as new rows or a separate table.\n   207→\n   208→---\n   209→\n   210→## 8. Note What Is Still Open\n   211→\n   212→Not everything needs to be resolved before coding. Note open questions\n   213→internally — they will go in `@Description` annotations in the Java code\n   214→(step 9), not in a separate markdown section.\n   215→\n   216→**Do not silently resolve ambiguities.** When two interpretations of a rule are\n   217→plausible, note both as an open question rather than picking one.\n   218→\n   219→---\n   220→\n   221→## 9. Write the `@TableTest` Code\n   222→\n   223→This is the only step that produces output. Everything above was analysis.\n   224→Write the complete Java test class now — each concern becomes a `@TableTest`\n   225→method. Follow the main skill's Table Design section for syntax, and its\n   226→Quality Checks for verification.\n   227→\n   228→For each `@TableTest` method:\n   229→- Add `@DisplayName` with a clear title derived from the concern name (step 1)\n   230→- Add `@Description` with context the table alone cannot convey — fixed values,\n   231→  formulas, domain context, or open questions from step 8\n   232→- Use `@TypeConverter` methods for any human-readable values that need conversion\n   233→- Follow annotation order: `@DisplayName` → `@Description` → `@TableTest`\n   234→\n   235→**Do not present markdown tables for review.** Do not add a notes section or\n   236→open questions list after the code. Go directly to the Java code. Open\n   237→questions from step 8 belong in `@Description` annotations inside the code.\n   238→\n   239→### Final checklist\n   240→\n   241→- [ ] Each concern has its own `@TableTest` method with a clear name\n   242→- [ ] Boundary values are tested at the threshold (not just mid-range values)\n   243→- [ ] Value sets group same-outcome values where applicable\n   244→- [ ] Rules and arithmetic are in separate `@TableTest` methods\n   245→- [ ] Stateful features use before/action/after columns\n   246→- [ ] Policy thresholds are visible as columns\n   247→- [ ] Open questions are in `@Description`, not silently resolved\n   248→- [ ] Each row is independently executable — no row depends on a prior row\n   249→- [ ] `@DisplayName`, `@Description`, `@TableTest` annotations in correct order\n   250→- [ ] `@TypeConverter` methods handle any non-standard type conversions\n   251→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f2761dc-ae31-4a8f-b4c8-a43716e269dc","uuid":"2347a5ff-125c-431d-903b-f672df3ab15f","timestamp":"2026-04-06T16:51:31.967Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/fz/zxx_qbvj49gbql83cc5l47pr0000gn/T/eval-skill-1775493393027/skills/tabletest/references/requirements-to-tables.md","content":"# From Requirements to Tables\n\nUse this reference when writing tests from natural-language requirements, vague\nfeature descriptions, or when it is not clear how to decompose the behaviour\ninto tables.\n\n**Your deliverable is a Java test class with `@TableTest` methods.**\nDo not output markdown tables. Do not stop for user feedback between steps.\nWork through every step below, then write the Java class directly as your\nfinal output.\n\nEach step below is an analysis step that shapes the table structure. As you\nwork through them, you are building up a mental model of the columns, rows,\nand concerns — but you do not output anything until step 9, where you write\nthe complete Java `@TableTest` class.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Name Each Concern\n\nStart by identifying the distinct behaviours in the requirement. Name each one\nas a verb phrase:\n\n- \"Calculate weekly pay\"\n- \"Determine discount tier\"\n- \"Check traveller eligibility\"\n- \"Validate coupon\"\n\n**If you cannot name a behaviour without using \"and\", it is two concerns** —\nsplit them. Each concern becomes a candidate for its own `@TableTest` method.\n\nSigns that concerns are mixed:\n- Some rows need columns that other rows leave blank throughout\n- Scenario names require qualifiers like \"...for eligibility\" vs \"...for pricing\"\n- The table has two groups of output columns that never both apply in the same row\n\n**Signs of a missing concern:**\n- An input to one rule is itself derived from raw data. The recognisable\n  shape: a rule takes a value that is computed via a time window, rolling\n  count, aggregation, or filter over raw records (e.g. \"average response\n  time over the last 7 days\" feeds into an SLA compliance check). The\n  derivation has its own edge cases — what falls inside vs outside the\n  window, how the boundary instant is handled, what records are included —\n  and these need boundary testing in a separate table. The rule table then\n  takes the derived value as a direct input column, not the raw data.\n\n  Two tables, not one: *Concern 1 — derive the value* (which raw records\n  count, window boundaries). *Concern 2 — apply the rule* (given the\n  derived value, what is the outcome?).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Find the First Example\n\nFor each concern, start with the simplest, most obvious case:\n\n- What does a typical successful case look like?\n- What are the concrete values — not abstractions, but real numbers, names, dates?\n\nWrite this as the first data row. Column naming can be rough at this stage.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Probe for Variations\n\nWork through these systematically:\n\n**Different outcomes** — what makes the decision go the other way?\n- \"What makes the answer change from yes to no?\"\n- \"What other rules apply?\"\n\n**Boundary conditions** — where exactly do rules trigger?\n- \"At what exact value does this rule kick in?\"\n- Include rows at the threshold, just above, and just below\n- When a boundary is ambiguous (inclusive vs exclusive?), add rows on both sides\n  AND mark the expected output as an open question — the rows should exist even\n  if the outcome is uncertain\n\n**Special cases** — situations that may surprise:\n- \"Is there a case that surprises new team members?\"\n- \"What is a common misunderstanding about this behaviour?\"\n\n**Absent inputs** — what happens when information is not provided?\n- \"What if this field is empty or not given?\"\n- \"Is there a sensible default, or does absence cause rejection?\"\n\n---\n\n## 4. Probe for Irrelevant Inputs\n\nWhen one input clearly does not affect the outcome, use a value set to express\n\"regardless of\":\n\n```\nScenario                        | Customer Age | Car Category       | Eligible?\nUnderage regardless of category | 17           | {Economy, Premium} | no\n```\n\nThis is more precise than a blank (which means absent) and more concise than\nseparate rows for each category.\n\nCheck every input dimension mentioned in the requirements. If the requirement\nsays \"regardless of X\", X must appear as a column with a value set — omitting\nit silently hides the assertion that X is irrelevant.\n\n**Common mistake — omitting the irrelevant column entirely.** If a requirement\nsays \"regardless of membership level\", the table must include a Membership\nLevel column with a value set like `{Gold, Silver, Bronze}`. Without it, a\nreader cannot tell whether the irrelevance was tested or simply overlooked.\nThe column makes the \"regardless of\" assertion visible and verifiable.\n\n**Value sets for tier grouping:** When multiple input values produce the same\noutput (a tier), group them into a value set rather than enumerating each as\na separate row:\n\n```\nScenario   | Credit Hours         | Standing?\nFreshman   | {0, 10, 20, 29}     | Freshman\nSophomore  | {30, 45, 59}        | Sophomore\nJunior     | {60, 75, 89}        | Junior\nSenior     | {90, 100, 120}      | Senior\n```\n\nThis makes the tier structure a first-class concept — each row IS a tier. The\nalternative — separate rows for boundaries (29 → Freshman, 30 → Sophomore) —\ntests boundary detection but obscures which values belong to the same tier. Use\nvalue sets for tier membership; add boundary rows (29 vs 30) only as additional\nrows alongside the tier rows, not instead of them.\n\n**Value sets for identical entity types:** When two categories follow\nidentical rules (e.g. `{Manager, Director}` both have the same expense\napproval limit), express them as a value set in one row rather than separate\nrows. This is more concise AND more expressive — it asserts the rules are\nidentical, whereas separate rows merely happen to show the same output.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Separate Rules from Arithmetic\n\nTables should specify the interesting decisions — classifications, eligibility\nrules, tier lookups, state transitions — not test that multiplication works.\n\n**Signs you are testing arithmetic, not rules:**\n- Every row has the same decision outcome but different numbers\n- The table is testing that `a * b = c` for various values of a and b\n- Removing rows would not lose any rule coverage\n\nWhen arithmetic is the only thing left to test, a small table (2-3 rows)\nverifying the formula is sufficient. The bulk of the table rows should cover the\ndomain rules.\n\n**Good decomposition** — separate the rule from the calculation:\n\nTable 1 — the rule (which tier?):\n```\nScenario     | Weight | Zone          | Category?  | Surcharge?\nLight domestic | 0.5  | Domestic      | Standard   | none\nHeavy domestic | 12   | Domestic      | Oversize   | 5.00\n```\n\nTable 2 — the arithmetic (what does it cost?):\n```\nScenario    | Base rate | Surcharge | Total?\nNo surcharge | 10.00    |           | 10.00\nWith surcharge | 10.00  | 5.00      | 15.00\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Frame Stateful Features as Rules\n\nWhen a feature involves state (carts, orders, workflows), frame each row as a\nstate transition rule — not a step in a sequential path:\n\n```\nScenario              | Cart before    | Action         | Cart after?    | Message?\nAdd to empty cart     | []             | add Widget     | [Widget x1]    | Added\nRemove last item      | [Widget x1]    | remove Widget  | []             | Removed\nRemove unknown item   | [Widget x1]    | remove Gadget  | [Widget x1]    | Not found\n```\n\nEach row is independent: given this state, when this action happens, expect this\nresult. No row depends on a previous row's outcome.\n\n**Include before and after columns** — even when the prompt describes the\noperation procedurally (\"customers can enter a coupon code\") rather than as a\nstate transition. Whenever an operation changes system state, the reader needs to\nsee what changed.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Make Thresholds Visible\n\nWhen a rule depends on a threshold or limit, include it as a column — even when\nthe value is constant across every row:\n\n```\nScenario            | Customer Age | Max Age (Policy) | Eligible?\nStandard customer   | 30           | 75               | yes\nAt the limit        | 75           | 75               | yes\nJust over the limit | 76           | 75               | no\n```\n\nA constant column often signals configuration. Ask: \"Under what circumstances\nwould this value differ?\" The answer may reveal a second axis (e.g., max age\nvaries by car category) that belongs as new rows or a separate table.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Note What Is Still Open\n\nNot everything needs to be resolved before coding. Note open questions\ninternally — they will go in `@Description` annotations in the Java code\n(step 9), not in a separate markdown section.\n\n**Do not silently resolve ambiguities.** When two interpretations of a rule are\nplausible, note both as an open question rather than picking one.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Write the `@TableTest` Code\n\nThis is the only step that produces output. Everything above was analysis.\nWrite the complete Java test class now — each concern becomes a `@TableTest`\nmethod. Follow the main skill's Table Design section for syntax, and its\nQuality Checks for verification.\n\nFor each `@TableTest` method:\n- Add `@DisplayName` with a clear title derived from the concern name (step 1)\n- Add `@Description` with context the table alone cannot convey — fixed values,\n  formulas, domain context, or open questions from step 8\n- Use `@TypeConverter` methods for any human-readable values that need conversion\n- Follow annotation order: `@DisplayName` → `@Description` → `@TableTest`\n\n**Do not present markdown tables for review.** Do not add a notes section or\nopen questions list after the code. Go directly to the Java code. Open\nquestions from step 8 belong in `@Description` annotations inside the code.\n\n### Final checklist\n\n- [ ] Each concern has its own `@TableTest` method with a clear name\n- [ ] Boundary values are tested at the threshold (not just mid-range values)\n- [ ] Value sets group same-outcome values where applicable\n- [ ] Rules and arithmetic are in separate `@TableTest` methods\n- [ ] Stateful features use before/action/after columns\n- [ ] Policy thresholds are visible as columns\n- [ ] Open questions are in `@Description`, not silently resolved\n- [ ] Each row is independently executable — no row depends on a prior row\n- [ ] `@DisplayName`, `@Description`, `@TableTest` annotations in correct order\n- [ ] `@TypeConverter` methods handle any non-standard type conversions\n","numLines":251,"startLine":1,"totalLines":251}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01U258tnV2pwJtxaSouJvrLp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QGWjLDhU5iN1Y84Tcx5Df7","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/folders/fz/zxx_qbvj49gbql83cc5l47pr0000gn/T/eval-skill-1775493393027/skills/tabletest/references/type-converters.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":28467,"cache_read_input_tokens":8311,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":28467,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f2761dc-ae31-4a8f-b4c8-a43716e269dc","uuid":"1995e031-a8f0-40f7-a457-a2f5cade7c01"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QGWjLDhU5iN1Y84Tcx5Df7","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Custom Type Converter Methods\n     2→\n     3→## Table of Contents\n     4→- [Prefer Built-in Conversion Before Writing a Converter](#prefer-built-in-conversion-before-writing-a-converter)\n     5→- [Writing Custom Converter Methods](#writing-custom-converter-methods) (Java / Kotlin)\n     6→- [Using @TypeConverterSources](#using-typeconvertersources)\n     7→- [Converter Method Requirements](#converter-method-requirements)\n     8→- [Search Strategy](#search-strategy)\n     9→- [Handling Null Values](#handling-null-values)\n    10→- [Domain-Specific Formatting](#domain-specific-formatting)\n    11→- [Converter Methods with Defaults](#converter-methods-with-defaults)\n    12→- [Common Wrapper Types](#common-wrapper-types)\n    13→\n    14→## Prefer Built-in Conversion Before Writing a Converter\n    15→\n    16→JUnit can convert strings to `Class<?>` when the value is a fully-qualified class name. Write `java.lang.RuntimeException` in the table instead of `RuntimeException` plus a custom `@TypeConverter`. This also works for collection elements: `[java.lang.RuntimeException, java.lang.NullPointerException]` → `List<Class<?>>`.\n    17→\n    18→Only write a converter method when built-in conversion does not cover the type.\n    19→\n    20→### Non-obvious built-in conversions\n    21→\n    22→The common types (primitives, `String`, `Path`, `URI`, `URL`, `UUID`, `LocalDate`, `LocalTime`, `LocalDateTime`) are supported as expected. Less obvious conversions worth knowing:\n    23→\n    24→| Table value | Parameter type | Notes |\n    25→|---|---|---|\n    26→| `\"SECONDS\"` | `TimeUnit` (any enum) | Enum name, case-sensitive |\n    27→| `\"0xF\"`, `\"017\"` | `int`/`long` (and boxed) | Hex and octal literals work |\n    28→| `\"java.lang.Integer\"` | `Class<?>` | Fully-qualified name required |\n    29→| `\"java.lang.Thread$State\"` | `Class<?>` | `$` for nested classes |\n    30→| `\"byte\"` | `Class<?>` | Primitive type names work |\n    31→| `\"char[]\"` | `Class<?>` | Array type names work |\n    32→| `\"PT3S\"`, `\"PT1H30M\"` | `Duration` | ISO 8601 duration format |\n    33→| `\"P2M6D\"` | `Period` | ISO 8601 period format |\n    34→| `\"JPY\"` | `Currency` | ISO 4217 currency code |\n    35→| `\"en-US\"` | `Locale` | IETF BCP 47 language tag |\n    36→\n    37→**Enums**: Write the enum constant name — `\"SECONDS\"` not `\"TimeUnit.SECONDS\"`. The parameter type tells JUnit which enum to use.\n    38→\n    39→```java\n    40→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    41→    Scenario       | Unit    | Duration?\n    42→    In seconds     | SECONDS | PT3S\n    43→    In minutes     | MINUTES | PT5M\n    44→    \"\"\")\n    45→void formats_duration(TimeUnit unit, Duration duration) { ... }\n    46→```\n    47→\n    48→---\n    49→\n    50→## Writing Custom Converter Methods\n    51→\n    52→When JUnit's built-in converters don't support your parameter type, add custom type converter methods annotated with `@TypeConverter`.\n    53→\n    54→## Java\n    55→\n    56→Place custom converter methods as `@TypeConverter`-annotated `public static` methods in **a public test class** or a class listed in `@TypeConverterSources`.\n    57→\n    58→**IMPORTANT:** The test class must be declared `public` for TableTest to discover converter methods:\n    59→```java\n    60→public class MyTest {  // Must be public, not package-private\n    61→    // Converter methods here will be found\n    62→}\n    63→```\n    64→\n    65→```java\n    66→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    67→    Date       | Days Until?\n    68→    today      | 0\n    69→    tomorrow   | 1\n    70→    \"\"\")\n    71→void testDaysUntil(LocalDate date, int expected) {\n    72→    assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date));\n    73→}\n    74→\n    75→@TypeConverter\n    76→public static LocalDate parseLocalDate(String input) {\n    77→    return switch (input) {\n    78→        case \"today\" -> LocalDate.now();\n    79→        case \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1);\n    80→        default -> LocalDate.parse(input);\n    81→    };\n    82→}\n    83→```\n    84→\n    85→## Kotlin\n    86→\n    87→For Kotlin tests, there are two ways to declare converter methods:\n    88→\n    89→### Option 1: Package-Level Functions\n    90→\n    91→Declare converter methods at package level in the same file as the test class:\n    92→\n    93→```kotlin\n    94→// At package level (top of file, outside class)\n    95→@TypeConverter\n    96→fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n    97→    \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n    98→    \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n    99→    else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n   100→}\n   101→\n   102→class DateTest {\n   103→    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n   104→        Date       | Days Until?\n   105→        today      | 0\n   106→        tomorrow   | 1\n   107→        \"\"\")\n   108→    fun testDaysUntil(date: LocalDate, expected: Int) {\n   109→        assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date))\n   110→    }\n   111→}\n   112→```\n   113→\n   114→### Option 2: Companion Object with @JvmStatic\n   115→\n   116→Declare converter methods in the companion object with `@JvmStatic` and `@TypeConverter`:\n   117→\n   118→```kotlin\n   119→class DateTest {\n   120→    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n   121→        Date       | Days Until?\n   122→        today      | 0\n   123→        tomorrow   | 1\n   124→        \"\"\")\n   125→    fun testDaysUntil(date: LocalDate, expected: Int) {\n   126→        assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date))\n   127→    }\n   128→\n   129→    companion object {\n   130→        @JvmStatic\n   131→        @TypeConverter\n   132→        fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n   133→            \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n   134→            \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n   135→            else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n   136→        }\n   137→    }\n   138→}\n   139→```\n   140→\n   141→**Note**: `@Nested` inner classes in Kotlin cannot have companion objects. Use package-level functions or outer class companion object instead.\n   142→\n   143→## Using @TypeConverterSources\n   144→\n   145→For shared converter methods across multiple test classes:\n   146→\n   147→**Java:**\n   148→```java\n   149→@TypeConverterSources(DateConverters.class)\n   150→class DateTest {\n   151→    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n   152→        ...\n   153→        \"\"\")\n   154→    void testWithSharedConverters(LocalDate date, Duration duration) { ... }\n   155→}\n   156→```\n   157→\n   158→**Kotlin** — use an `object` declaration with `@JvmStatic` and `@TypeConverter`:\n   159→```kotlin\n   160→object DateConverters {\n   161→    @JvmStatic\n   162→    @TypeConverter\n   163→    fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n   164→        \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n   165→        \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n   166→        else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n   167→    }\n   168→}\n   169→\n   170→@TypeConverterSources(DateConverters::class)\n   171→class DateTest {\n   172→    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n   173→        ...\n   174→        \"\"\")\n   175→    fun testWithSharedConverters(date: LocalDate, duration: Duration) { ... }\n   176→}\n   177→```\n   178→\n   179→## Converter Method Requirements\n   180→\n   181→A converter method will be used when it:\n   182→1. Is annotated with `@TypeConverter`\n   183→2. Is defined as a `public static` method in a `public class`\n   184→3. Accepts exactly one parameter\n   185→4. Returns an object of the target parameter type\n   186→5. **Is the only `@TypeConverter` method matching the above criteria in the class**\n   187→\n   188→There is no specific naming pattern required, but `parse<TypeName>` (e.g., `parseLocalDate`, `parseMoney`) is conventional.\n   189→\n   190→### One Converter Method Per Target Type\n   191→\n   192→You cannot have multiple converter methods with the same target type (same return type), even with different names:\n   193→\n   194→```java\n   195→// WRONG - TableTest cannot determine which to use\n   196→@TypeConverter\n   197→public static Map<String, String> parseRequest(String value) { ... }\n   198→@TypeConverter\n   199→public static Map<String, String> parseResponse(String value) { ... }\n   200→\n   201→// CORRECT - Single converter handles both formats\n   202→@TypeConverter\n   203→public static Map<String, String> parseRequestOrResponse(String value) {\n   204→    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n   205→        // Handle response format\n   206→    } else {\n   207→        // Handle request format\n   208→    }\n   209→    return ...;\n   210→}\n   211→```\n   212→\n   213→**Even better - split into separate columns instead:**\n   214→```java\n   215→// BEST - No converter method needed\n   216→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   217→    Request Status | Request ms | Response Status? | Response ms?\n   218→    OK             | 10         | OK               | <50\n   219→    \"\"\")\n   220→void test(String reqStatus, Integer reqMs, String respStatus, Long respMs) {\n   221→    // Use parseResponseTime converter for respMs to handle \"<50\" format\n   222→}\n   223→\n   224→@TypeConverter\n   225→public static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n   226→    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n   227→        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n   228→    }\n   229→    return Long.parseLong(value);\n   230→}\n   231→```\n   232→\n   233→## Search Strategy\n   234→\n   235→TableTest searches for converter methods in this order (stops at first match):\n   236→\n   237→**Java:**\n   238→1. Current test class (including inherited methods)\n   239→2. Enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests, starting with direct outer class)\n   240→3. Classes listed in `@TypeConverterSources` (in order listed)\n   241→4. `@TypeConverterSources` of enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests)\n   242→\n   243→**Kotlin:**\n   244→1. Current file (package-level functions and outer class companion object)\n   245→2. Classes listed in `@TypeConverterSources` (in order listed)\n   246→3. `@TypeConverterSources` of enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests)\n   247→\n   248→If a converter method isn't being found, check that it meets the requirements above and is in a location that matches the search order.\n   249→\n   250→## Handling Null Values\n   251→\n   252→When table cells can be blank (representing null), use appropriate return types:\n   253→\n   254→### Primitive vs Boxed Types\n   255→\n   256→```java\n   257→// WRONG - primitives cannot be null\n   258→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   259→    Value | Time\n   260→    OK    | 10\n   261→          |        <- Blank cell causes error: \"null cannot be assigned to primitive type long\"\n   262→    \"\"\")\n   263→void test(String value, long time) { ... }\n   264→\n   265→// CORRECT - use boxed type\n   266→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   267→    Value | Time\n   268→    OK    | 10\n   269→          |        <- Blank cell becomes null\n   270→    \"\"\")\n   271→void test(String value, Long time) { ... }\n   272→```\n   273→\n   274→### Converter Methods with Null Input\n   275→\n   276→Converter methods receive `null` for blank cells. Handle this appropriately:\n   277→\n   278→```java\n   279→// Return null for blank input\n   280→@TypeConverter\n   281→public static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n   282→    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n   283→        return null;  // Blank cell -> null value\n   284→    }\n   285→    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n   286→        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n   287→    }\n   288→    return Long.parseLong(value);\n   289→}\n   290→\n   291→// Return empty collection for blank input\n   292→@TypeConverter\n   293→public static List<String> parseItems(String value) {\n   294→    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n   295→        return List.of();  // Blank cell -> empty list\n   296→    }\n   297→    return Arrays.asList(value.split(\",\"));\n   298→}\n   299→```\n   300→\n   301→**Common pattern:** Blank cells in the table map to null/empty in the domain.\n   302→\n   303→## Domain-Specific Formatting\n   304→\n   305→Converter methods enable domain-specific formatting conventions that improve table readability:\n   306→\n   307→```java\n   308→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   309→    Scenario     | Response Time?\n   310→    Fast         | <50\n   311→    Acceptable   | <150\n   312→    Slow         | <500\n   313→    \"\"\")\n   314→void testResponseTime(Long maxResponseTimeMs) {\n   315→    // maxResponseTimeMs is 50, 150, 500 respectively\n   316→}\n   317→\n   318→@TypeConverter\n   319→public static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n   320→    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n   321→        return null;\n   322→    }\n   323→    // Handle \"less than\" notation\n   324→    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n   325→        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n   326→    }\n   327→    return Long.parseLong(value);\n   328→}\n   329→```\n   330→\n   331→**Benefits:**\n   332→- `<50` clearly communicates \"less than 50ms\" in the table\n   333→- Converter handles the formatting detail\n   334→- Table is self-documenting without code comments\n   335→\n   336→**Other examples:**\n   337→- Duration: `5m`, `30s` -> milliseconds\n   338→- Currency: `$100`, `EUR50` -> numeric values\n   339→- Percentage: `50%` -> 0.5\n   340→- File size: `10KB`, `5MB` -> bytes\n   341→\n   342→## Converter Methods with Defaults\n   343→\n   344→Converter methods can provide sensible defaults for missing map entries, making tables clearer when properties vary by scenario:\n   345→\n   346→```java\n   347→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   348→    Scenario          | Config                                  | Expected?\n   349→    Basic request     | [method: GET]                           | OK\n   350→    With timeout      | [method: POST, timeout: 5000]           | OK\n   351→    Full config       | [method: POST, timeout: 5000, retry: 3] | OK\n   352→    \"\"\")\n   353→void testRequest(Map<String, String> config, String expected) {\n   354→    RequestConfig request = buildRequestConfig(config);\n   355→    assertEquals(expected, process(request));\n   356→}\n   357→\n   358→@TypeConverter\n   359→public static RequestConfig buildRequestConfig(Map<String, String> config) {\n   360→    if (config == null || config.isEmpty()) {\n   361→        return RequestConfig.defaults();\n   362→    }\n   363→    return new RequestConfig(\n   364→        config.getOrDefault(\"method\", \"GET\"),           // Default: GET\n   365→        parseInt(config.getOrDefault(\"timeout\", \"3000\")), // Default: 3000ms\n   366→        config.get(\"auth\"),                             // Default: null (no auth)\n   367→        parseInt(config.getOrDefault(\"retry\", \"1\")),     // Default: 1 retry\n   368→        parseBoolean(config.getOrDefault(\"cache\", \"false\")) // Default: no cache\n   369→    );\n   370→}\n   371→```\n   372→\n   373→**Benefits:**\n   374→- Each scenario only specifies what varies from defaults\n   375→- Table focuses on the interesting variations\n   376→- Defaults are documented in one place (converter method)\n   377→- No sea of blank columns\n   378→\n   379→**When to use:**\n   380→- Different scenarios need different subsets of properties\n   381→- Most properties have sensible default values\n   382→- Alternative is many blank cells in separate columns\n   383→\n   384→**When not to use:**\n   385→- All scenarios use the same properties (use separate columns instead)\n   386→- No sensible defaults exist (every property is required)\n   387→\n   388→## Common Wrapper Types\n   389→\n   390→JUnit's built-in converters don't handle wrapper types like `Optional`, `Result`, or `Either`. Converter methods are needed for these common patterns.\n   391→\n   392→### Optional<T>\n   393→\n   394→```java\n   395→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   396→    Scenario        | Feature Toggles      | Result?\n   397→    Feature enabled | [feature-x: true]    | true\n   398→    Not registered  | [:]                  | empty\n   399→    Feature disabled| [feature-x: false]   | false\n   400→    \"\"\")\n   401→void testFeatureToggle(Map<String, Boolean> toggles, Optional<Boolean> result) {\n   402→    assertEquals(result, featureResolver.find(\"feature-x\", toggles));\n   403→}\n   404→\n   405→@TypeConverter\n   406→public static Optional<Boolean> result(String value) {\n   407→    return switch (value) {\n   408→        case \"empty\" -> Optional.empty();\n   409→        case \"true\" -> Optional.of(true);\n   410→        case \"false\" -> Optional.of(false);\n   411→        default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown result: \" + value);\n   412→    };\n   413→}\n   414→```\n   415→\n   416→**Pattern**: Use simple string representations (`empty`, `true`, `false`) rather than trying to parse `Optional.empty()` syntax.\n   417→\n   418→### Optional<String>\n   419→\n   420→```java\n   421→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   422→    Scenario     | Input  | Result?\n   423→    Found        | active | active\n   424→    Not found    | unused | empty\n   425→    \"\"\")\n   426→void testLookup(String input, Optional<String> result) {\n   427→    assertEquals(result, lookup(input));\n   428→}\n   429→\n   430→@TypeConverter\n   431→public static Optional<String> result(String value) {\n   432→    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n   433→}\n   434→```\n   435→\n   436→### Result<T, E> (or Either<L, R>)\n   437→\n   438→For Result/Either types, use string notation to indicate success vs failure:\n   439→\n   440→```java\n   441→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   442→    Scenario       | Input | Result?\n   443→    Valid input    | 42    | OK\n   444→    Invalid input  | abc   | ERROR: Not a number\n   445→    Out of range   | -1    | ERROR: Must be positive\n   446→    \"\"\")\n   447→void testValidation(String input, Result<Integer, String> result) {\n   448→    assertEquals(result, validator.validate(input));\n   449→}\n   450→\n   451→@TypeConverter\n   452→public static Result<Integer, String> result(String value) {\n   453→    if (value.equals(\"OK\")) {\n   454→        return Result.ok(42);  // Use value from Input column\n   455→    }\n   456→    if (value.startsWith(\"ERROR: \")) {\n   457→        return Result.error(value.substring(7));\n   458→    }\n   459→    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown result: \" + value);\n   460→}\n   461→```\n   462→\n   463→**Pattern**: Prefix error cases with `ERROR:` to distinguish from success cases.\n   464→\n   465→**Better approach**: Use separate columns for success value and error message:\n   466→\n   467→```java\n   468→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   469→    Scenario       | Input | Success? | Error?\n   470→    Valid input    | 42    | 42       |\n   471→    Invalid input  | abc   |          | Not a number\n   472→    Out of range   | -1    |          | Must be positive\n   473→    \"\"\")\n   474→void testValidation(String input, Integer success, String error) {\n   475→    Result<Integer, String> expected = success != null\n   476→        ? Result.ok(success)\n   477→        : Result.error(error);\n   478→    assertEquals(expected, validator.validate(input));\n   479→}\n   480→```\n   481→\n   482→This avoids the converter method entirely and makes the table clearer.\n   483→\n   484→### Multiple Optional Parameters\n   485→\n   486→When you have multiple `Optional` parameters in the same test, use different column naming:\n   487→\n   488→```java\n   489→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   490→    Scenario          | Primary?  | Fallback?\n   491→    Both present      | primary   | fallback\n   492→    Primary only      | primary   | empty\n   493→    Fallback only     | empty     | fallback\n   494→    Neither present   | empty     | empty\n   495→    \"\"\")\n   496→void testOptionals(Optional<String> primary, Optional<String> fallback) {\n   497→    // Test with both optionals\n   498→}\n   499→\n   500→@TypeConverter\n   501→public static Optional<String> primary(String value) {\n   502→    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n   503→}\n   504→\n   505→@TypeConverter\n   506→public static Optional<String> fallback(String value) {\n   507→    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n   508→}\n   509→```\n   510→\n   511→**Important**: Column name matching works here. The converter method name should match the parameter name, and TableTest will use the appropriate converter for each column.\n   512→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f2761dc-ae31-4a8f-b4c8-a43716e269dc","uuid":"eb0cdbda-cf54-462a-baca-3ba895af351f","timestamp":"2026-04-06T16:51:32.427Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/fz/zxx_qbvj49gbql83cc5l47pr0000gn/T/eval-skill-1775493393027/skills/tabletest/references/type-converters.md","content":"# Custom Type Converter Methods\n\n## Table of Contents\n- [Prefer Built-in Conversion Before Writing a Converter](#prefer-built-in-conversion-before-writing-a-converter)\n- [Writing Custom Converter Methods](#writing-custom-converter-methods) (Java / Kotlin)\n- [Using @TypeConverterSources](#using-typeconvertersources)\n- [Converter Method Requirements](#converter-method-requirements)\n- [Search Strategy](#search-strategy)\n- [Handling Null Values](#handling-null-values)\n- [Domain-Specific Formatting](#domain-specific-formatting)\n- [Converter Methods with Defaults](#converter-methods-with-defaults)\n- [Common Wrapper Types](#common-wrapper-types)\n\n## Prefer Built-in Conversion Before Writing a Converter\n\nJUnit can convert strings to `Class<?>` when the value is a fully-qualified class name. Write `java.lang.RuntimeException` in the table instead of `RuntimeException` plus a custom `@TypeConverter`. This also works for collection elements: `[java.lang.RuntimeException, java.lang.NullPointerException]` → `List<Class<?>>`.\n\nOnly write a converter method when built-in conversion does not cover the type.\n\n### Non-obvious built-in conversions\n\nThe common types (primitives, `String`, `Path`, `URI`, `URL`, `UUID`, `LocalDate`, `LocalTime`, `LocalDateTime`) are supported as expected. Less obvious conversions worth knowing:\n\n| Table value | Parameter type | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\"SECONDS\"` | `TimeUnit` (any enum) | Enum name, case-sensitive |\n| `\"0xF\"`, `\"017\"` | `int`/`long` (and boxed) | Hex and octal literals work |\n| `\"java.lang.Integer\"` | `Class<?>` | Fully-qualified name required |\n| `\"java.lang.Thread$State\"` | `Class<?>` | `$` for nested classes |\n| `\"byte\"` | `Class<?>` | Primitive type names work |\n| `\"char[]\"` | `Class<?>` | Array type names work |\n| `\"PT3S\"`, `\"PT1H30M\"` | `Duration` | ISO 8601 duration format |\n| `\"P2M6D\"` | `Period` | ISO 8601 period format |\n| `\"JPY\"` | `Currency` | ISO 4217 currency code |\n| `\"en-US\"` | `Locale` | IETF BCP 47 language tag |\n\n**Enums**: Write the enum constant name — `\"SECONDS\"` not `\"TimeUnit.SECONDS\"`. The parameter type tells JUnit which enum to use.\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario       | Unit    | Duration?\n    In seconds     | SECONDS | PT3S\n    In minutes     | MINUTES | PT5M\n    \"\"\")\nvoid formats_duration(TimeUnit unit, Duration duration) { ... }\n```\n\n---\n\n## Writing Custom Converter Methods\n\nWhen JUnit's built-in converters don't support your parameter type, add custom type converter methods annotated with `@TypeConverter`.\n\n## Java\n\nPlace custom converter methods as `@TypeConverter`-annotated `public static` methods in **a public test class** or a class listed in `@TypeConverterSources`.\n\n**IMPORTANT:** The test class must be declared `public` for TableTest to discover converter methods:\n```java\npublic class MyTest {  // Must be public, not package-private\n    // Converter methods here will be found\n}\n```\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Date       | Days Until?\n    today      | 0\n    tomorrow   | 1\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testDaysUntil(LocalDate date, int expected) {\n    assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static LocalDate parseLocalDate(String input) {\n    return switch (input) {\n        case \"today\" -> LocalDate.now();\n        case \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1);\n        default -> LocalDate.parse(input);\n    };\n}\n```\n\n## Kotlin\n\nFor Kotlin tests, there are two ways to declare converter methods:\n\n### Option 1: Package-Level Functions\n\nDeclare converter methods at package level in the same file as the test class:\n\n```kotlin\n// At package level (top of file, outside class)\n@TypeConverter\nfun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n    \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n    \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n    else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n}\n\nclass DateTest {\n    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n        Date       | Days Until?\n        today      | 0\n        tomorrow   | 1\n        \"\"\")\n    fun testDaysUntil(date: LocalDate, expected: Int) {\n        assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Option 2: Companion Object with @JvmStatic\n\nDeclare converter methods in the companion object with `@JvmStatic` and `@TypeConverter`:\n\n```kotlin\nclass DateTest {\n    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n        Date       | Days Until?\n        today      | 0\n        tomorrow   | 1\n        \"\"\")\n    fun testDaysUntil(date: LocalDate, expected: Int) {\n        assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date))\n    }\n\n    companion object {\n        @JvmStatic\n        @TypeConverter\n        fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n            \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n            \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n            else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Note**: `@Nested` inner classes in Kotlin cannot have companion objects. Use package-level functions or outer class companion object instead.\n\n## Using @TypeConverterSources\n\nFor shared converter methods across multiple test classes:\n\n**Java:**\n```java\n@TypeConverterSources(DateConverters.class)\nclass DateTest {\n    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n        ...\n        \"\"\")\n    void testWithSharedConverters(LocalDate date, Duration duration) { ... }\n}\n```\n\n**Kotlin** — use an `object` declaration with `@JvmStatic` and `@TypeConverter`:\n```kotlin\nobject DateConverters {\n    @JvmStatic\n    @TypeConverter\n    fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n        \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n        \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n        else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n    }\n}\n\n@TypeConverterSources(DateConverters::class)\nclass DateTest {\n    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n        ...\n        \"\"\")\n    fun testWithSharedConverters(date: LocalDate, duration: Duration) { ... }\n}\n```\n\n## Converter Method Requirements\n\nA converter method will be used when it:\n1. Is annotated with `@TypeConverter`\n2. Is defined as a `public static` method in a `public class`\n3. Accepts exactly one parameter\n4. Returns an object of the target parameter type\n5. **Is the only `@TypeConverter` method matching the above criteria in the class**\n\nThere is no specific naming pattern required, but `parse<TypeName>` (e.g., `parseLocalDate`, `parseMoney`) is conventional.\n\n### One Converter Method Per Target Type\n\nYou cannot have multiple converter methods with the same target type (same return type), even with different names:\n\n```java\n// WRONG - TableTest cannot determine which to use\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Map<String, String> parseRequest(String value) { ... }\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Map<String, String> parseResponse(String value) { ... }\n\n// CORRECT - Single converter handles both formats\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Map<String, String> parseRequestOrResponse(String value) {\n    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n        // Handle response format\n    } else {\n        // Handle request format\n    }\n    return ...;\n}\n```\n\n**Even better - split into separate columns instead:**\n```java\n// BEST - No converter method needed\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Request Status | Request ms | Response Status? | Response ms?\n    OK             | 10         | OK               | <50\n    \"\"\")\nvoid test(String reqStatus, Integer reqMs, String respStatus, Long respMs) {\n    // Use parseResponseTime converter for respMs to handle \"<50\" format\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n    }\n    return Long.parseLong(value);\n}\n```\n\n## Search Strategy\n\nTableTest searches for converter methods in this order (stops at first match):\n\n**Java:**\n1. Current test class (including inherited methods)\n2. Enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests, starting with direct outer class)\n3. Classes listed in `@TypeConverterSources` (in order listed)\n4. `@TypeConverterSources` of enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests)\n\n**Kotlin:**\n1. Current file (package-level functions and outer class companion object)\n2. Classes listed in `@TypeConverterSources` (in order listed)\n3. `@TypeConverterSources` of enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests)\n\nIf a converter method isn't being found, check that it meets the requirements above and is in a location that matches the search order.\n\n## Handling Null Values\n\nWhen table cells can be blank (representing null), use appropriate return types:\n\n### Primitive vs Boxed Types\n\n```java\n// WRONG - primitives cannot be null\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Value | Time\n    OK    | 10\n          |        <- Blank cell causes error: \"null cannot be assigned to primitive type long\"\n    \"\"\")\nvoid test(String value, long time) { ... }\n\n// CORRECT - use boxed type\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Value | Time\n    OK    | 10\n          |        <- Blank cell becomes null\n    \"\"\")\nvoid test(String value, Long time) { ... }\n```\n\n### Converter Methods with Null Input\n\nConverter methods receive `null` for blank cells. Handle this appropriately:\n\n```java\n// Return null for blank input\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n        return null;  // Blank cell -> null value\n    }\n    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n    }\n    return Long.parseLong(value);\n}\n\n// Return empty collection for blank input\n@TypeConverter\npublic static List<String> parseItems(String value) {\n    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n        return List.of();  // Blank cell -> empty list\n    }\n    return Arrays.asList(value.split(\",\"));\n}\n```\n\n**Common pattern:** Blank cells in the table map to null/empty in the domain.\n\n## Domain-Specific Formatting\n\nConverter methods enable domain-specific formatting conventions that improve table readability:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario     | Response Time?\n    Fast         | <50\n    Acceptable   | <150\n    Slow         | <500\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testResponseTime(Long maxResponseTimeMs) {\n    // maxResponseTimeMs is 50, 150, 500 respectively\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    // Handle \"less than\" notation\n    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n    }\n    return Long.parseLong(value);\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- `<50` clearly communicates \"less than 50ms\" in the table\n- Converter handles the formatting detail\n- Table is self-documenting without code comments\n\n**Other examples:**\n- Duration: `5m`, `30s` -> milliseconds\n- Currency: `$100`, `EUR50` -> numeric values\n- Percentage: `50%` -> 0.5\n- File size: `10KB`, `5MB` -> bytes\n\n## Converter Methods with Defaults\n\nConverter methods can provide sensible defaults for missing map entries, making tables clearer when properties vary by scenario:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario          | Config                                  | Expected?\n    Basic request     | [method: GET]                           | OK\n    With timeout      | [method: POST, timeout: 5000]           | OK\n    Full config       | [method: POST, timeout: 5000, retry: 3] | OK\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testRequest(Map<String, String> config, String expected) {\n    RequestConfig request = buildRequestConfig(config);\n    assertEquals(expected, process(request));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static RequestConfig buildRequestConfig(Map<String, String> config) {\n    if (config == null || config.isEmpty()) {\n        return RequestConfig.defaults();\n    }\n    return new RequestConfig(\n        config.getOrDefault(\"method\", \"GET\"),           // Default: GET\n        parseInt(config.getOrDefault(\"timeout\", \"3000\")), // Default: 3000ms\n        config.get(\"auth\"),                             // Default: null (no auth)\n        parseInt(config.getOrDefault(\"retry\", \"1\")),     // Default: 1 retry\n        parseBoolean(config.getOrDefault(\"cache\", \"false\")) // Default: no cache\n    );\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- Each scenario only specifies what varies from defaults\n- Table focuses on the interesting variations\n- Defaults are documented in one place (converter method)\n- No sea of blank columns\n\n**When to use:**\n- Different scenarios need different subsets of properties\n- Most properties have sensible default values\n- Alternative is many blank cells in separate columns\n\n**When not to use:**\n- All scenarios use the same properties (use separate columns instead)\n- No sensible defaults exist (every property is required)\n\n## Common Wrapper Types\n\nJUnit's built-in converters don't handle wrapper types like `Optional`, `Result`, or `Either`. Converter methods are needed for these common patterns.\n\n### Optional<T>\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario        | Feature Toggles      | Result?\n    Feature enabled | [feature-x: true]    | true\n    Not registered  | [:]                  | empty\n    Feature disabled| [feature-x: false]   | false\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testFeatureToggle(Map<String, Boolean> toggles, Optional<Boolean> result) {\n    assertEquals(result, featureResolver.find(\"feature-x\", toggles));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Optional<Boolean> result(String value) {\n    return switch (value) {\n        case \"empty\" -> Optional.empty();\n        case \"true\" -> Optional.of(true);\n        case \"false\" -> Optional.of(false);\n        default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown result: \" + value);\n    };\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern**: Use simple string representations (`empty`, `true`, `false`) rather than trying to parse `Optional.empty()` syntax.\n\n### Optional<String>\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario     | Input  | Result?\n    Found        | active | active\n    Not found    | unused | empty\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testLookup(String input, Optional<String> result) {\n    assertEquals(result, lookup(input));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Optional<String> result(String value) {\n    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n}\n```\n\n### Result<T, E> (or Either<L, R>)\n\nFor Result/Either types, use string notation to indicate success vs failure:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario       | Input | Result?\n    Valid input    | 42    | OK\n    Invalid input  | abc   | ERROR: Not a number\n    Out of range   | -1    | ERROR: Must be positive\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testValidation(String input, Result<Integer, String> result) {\n    assertEquals(result, validator.validate(input));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Result<Integer, String> result(String value) {\n    if (value.equals(\"OK\")) {\n        return Result.ok(42);  // Use value from Input column\n    }\n    if (value.startsWith(\"ERROR: \")) {\n        return Result.error(value.substring(7));\n    }\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown result: \" + value);\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern**: Prefix error cases with `ERROR:` to distinguish from success cases.\n\n**Better approach**: Use separate columns for success value and error message:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario       | Input | Success? | Error?\n    Valid input    | 42    | 42       |\n    Invalid input  | abc   |          | Not a number\n    Out of range   | -1    |          | Must be positive\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testValidation(String input, Integer success, String error) {\n    Result<Integer, String> expected = success != null\n        ? Result.ok(success)\n        : Result.error(error);\n    assertEquals(expected, validator.validate(input));\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids the converter method entirely and makes the table clearer.\n\n### Multiple Optional Parameters\n\nWhen you have multiple `Optional` parameters in the same test, use different column naming:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario          | Primary?  | Fallback?\n    Both present      | primary   | fallback\n    Primary only      | primary   | empty\n    Fallback only     | empty     | fallback\n    Neither present   | empty     | empty\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testOptionals(Optional<String> primary, Optional<String> fallback) {\n    // Test with both optionals\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Optional<String> primary(String value) {\n    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Optional<String> fallback(String value) {\n    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n}\n```\n\n**Important**: Column name matching works here. The converter method name should match the parameter name, and TableTest will use the appropriate converter for each column.\n","numLines":512,"startLine":1,"totalLines":512}}}

